{"lecture_id":"2026-07-09-M2-3","lecture_summary":"Torque geometry and direction—moment arms, force angle, and the right-hand rule—followed by fixed-axis rotational dynamics using extended free-body diagrams, Στ = Iα, and additive moments of inertia. Examples include a door, an angled wrench, a cord-driven spool, and rigidly attached cylinders.","videos":[{"rank":1,"title":"Torque, Basic Introduction, Lever Arm, Moment of Force, Simple Machines & Mechanical Advantage","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jg4e8W44_E4","reason":"Establishes torque, lever arms, pivot distance, and rotational signs through leverage examples analogous to the lecture’s door and wrench problems."},{"rank":2,"title":"Inertia - Basic Introduction, Torque, Angular Acceleration, Newton's Second Law, Rotational Motion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ic_wFYu8xVs","reason":"Connects torque with angular acceleration through rotational Newton’s second law and explains how mass distribution determines moments of inertia, including disks."},{"rank":3,"title":"Rotational Dynamics - Basic Introduction","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dbvr-L5rxdg","reason":"Adds applied practice with tension and rotating pulleys, providing the closest playlist analogue to the spool problem and its free-body-diagram/torque-equation workflow."}],"coverage_gaps":["No strong mechanics match explicitly covers the cross-product right-hand rule and into/out-of-page torque notation.","The exact wrench strategy of finding the included angle from complementary geometry, plus line-of-action geometry d = r sin θ, is not confirmed by the playlist descriptions.","No strong exact match covers a fixed-spindle spool where a nonzero pivot force has zero torque, or rigidly attached coaxial cylinders whose moments of inertia are added to solve for an unknown mass.","Symbolic-first algebra and explicit dimensional checks are not specifically represented."]}
